  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nA low pressure system is slowly pushing southeast through the\nMidwest US as we head into this weekend. AT the surface, a lifting\nwarm front is the focus for scattered showers and thunderstorms\nthis afternoon. PWAT values in excess of 2.4 inches were analyzed\nalong this boundary today. It is hot and humid, to say the least.\nWidespread heat index values in the 105-110 degree range were\nbeing observed this afternoon, and a Heat Advisory remains in\neffect until early this evening. The near-moist adiabatic lapse\nrates aloft are a limiting factor for microburst production today,\nespecially in the plume of deepest tropical moisture. Cannot rule\nout a few stronger storms, but no organized risk for severe storms\nthis afternoon.\n\nTomorrow into Sunday, the low pressure system's cold front will\npush southeast into our area. The front will still serve as a\npotential focus for shower and thunderstorm development, but most\nnoticeable impact will be the \"drier\" (near 65-70 degree) dew\npoints wrapping into the region. By Tuesday/Wednesday the low will\npull to the northeast and get absorbed in the jet stream, leaving\nbroad zonal ridging along the Gulf Coast and interior South. It's\nnot the most dominant ridge, but will generally favor heat and\nlower rain chances through the mid to late next week. Expect a\nreturn of heat stress messaging when that occurs. /NF/",
